name: interfaces and addresses
boss: Netcat
flavor: I listen where you cannot see.
brief: An interface is a way onto a network. ip is the modern tool for all of
brief: it -- addresses, links, routes, neighbours -- and ifconfig and route are
brief: the older ones you will still meet on machines that have not moved on.
brief:
brief: ip has a shape: ip OBJECT COMMAND. ip addr, ip link, ip route, ip neigh.
brief: Every one of them takes show, add and del.
---
q: show every interface and its addresses
a: ip addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show addresses one per line, in a form that is easy to cut up
a: ip -o addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show only ipv4 addresses
a: ip -4 addr
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show the links, without their addresses
a: ip link
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: bring the interface @iface@ up
a: ip link set @iface@ up
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: bring @iface@ back down
a: ip link set @iface@ down
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: give @iface@ the address @ip@ with a 24 bit netmask
a: ip addr add @ip@/24 dev @iface@
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: take that address off @iface@ again
a: ip addr del @ip@/24 dev @iface@
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show the routing table
a: ip route
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show only the default route
a: ip route show default
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: add a default route via @ip@
a: ip route add default via @ip@
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: delete the default route
a: ip route del default
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show the arp neighbour table using ip
a: ip neigh
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: print just the interface names, one per line
a: ip -o link | cut -d: -f2
out: .
ref: ip
teach: ip
---
q: show interfaces the old way
a: ifconfig
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
---
q: show @iface@ specifically, the old way
a: ifconfig @iface@
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
---
q: set @iface@ up with an address, the old way
a: ifconfig @iface@ @ip@ up
ref: ifconfig
teach: ifconfig
---
q: show the routing table the old way
a: route
ref: route
teach: route
---
q: show routes without resolving names to make it fast
a: route -n
ref: route
teach: route -n
---
q: show route table entries with extended fields
a: route -e
ref: route
teach: route -e
---
q: show routes for a named address family
a: route -A inet
ref: route
teach: route -A
---
q: show the arp cache
a: arp
ref: arp
teach: arp
---
q: show the arp cache numerically, without resolving names
a: arp -n
ref: arp
teach: arp -n
---
q: show all arp entries including incomplete ones
a: arp -a
ref: arp
teach: arp -a
---
q: delete the arp entry for @ip@
a: arp -d @ip@
ref: arp
teach: arp -d
---
q: add a permanent arp entry
a: arp -s @ip@ 00:11:22:33:44:55
ref: arp
teach: arp -s
---
q: show arp entries for one interface only
a: arp -i @iface@
ref: arp
teach: arp -i
---
q: show the arp cache verbosely
a: arp -v
ref: arp
teach: arp -v
---
q: show arp entries of a given hardware type
a: arp -H ether
ref: arp
teach: arp -H
---
q: use a named address family for arp
a: arp -A inet
ref: arp
teach: arp -A
---
q: publish an arp entry on behalf of another host
a: arp -D @iface@ -s @ip@
ref: arp
teach: arp -D
---
q: is anyone already using the address @ip@ -- ask at the link layer
a: arping -c 3 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -c
hint: arp works below ip, so this finds hosts that will not answer a ping
---
q: send the arp probe out of a specific interface
a: arping -I @iface@ -c 3 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -I
---
q: arping quietly, reporting only the exit status
a: arping -q -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -q
---
q: send a duplicate-address-detection probe
a: arping -D -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -D
---
q: send an unsolicited arp announcement of your own address
a: arping -U -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -U
---
q: arping using a chosen source address
a: arping -s @ip2@ -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -s
---
q: stop arping after 5 seconds whatever happens
a: arping -w 5 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -w
---
q: keep arping until a reply arrives, then stop
a: arping -f @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -f
---
q: send broadcast arp probes
a: arping -b -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -b
---
q: arping in a mode that does not require a source address
a: arping -A -c 1 @ip@
ref: arping
teach: arping -A
---
q: work out the network settings for the address @ip@/24
a: ipcalc @ip@/24
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc
---
q: show just the broadcast address for @ip@/24
a: ipcalc -b @ip@/24
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -b
---
q: show just the network address for @ip@/24
a: ipcalc -n @ip@/24
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -n
---
q: show just the netmask for a /24
a: ipcalc -m @ip@/24
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -m
---
q: show the prefix length for a given netmask
a: ipcalc -p @ip@ 255.255.255.0
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -p
---
q: get ipcalc output without any resolved hostnames, for a script
a: ipcalc -s @ip@/24
ref: ipcalc
teach: ipcalc -s
